#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106768} :activity/id #uuid "515492e4-a1e3-5a32-b6be-2b3b52ae8e6b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327 :topic/id #uuid "4036942d-b5d5-592a-a620-1e02dcc440fc" :topic/math-academy-id 1388} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105657 :question/id #uuid "9a5697c7-0aa6-55d9-898c-6e954960df42" :question/math-academy-id "e-7388"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f8fd8a4-1ca2-521d-8542-d11311bcc586" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371187}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Variance Using the Definition Given a Probability Mass Function"} :step/id #uuid "028fa34b-a8a4-59cc-9914-96d5fd012ef1" :step/math-academy-id 27395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105658 :question/id #uuid "ee9c8798-408e-507a-8205-8f16c672c767" :question/math-academy-id "e-7390"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e448e28-466c-5051-9ada-5e030e6a8ff7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382545}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "6c8ce025-dadf-5d0d-8ee0-545c8dfe0d11" :step/math-academy-id 27397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107104 :tutorial/content "The **variance** of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} (x - \\textrm E[X])^2 \\cdot f(x).$$\n\nIn other words, the variance is the expected value of the squared difference between $X$ and the expected value of $X{:}$\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]$$\n\nThe variance quantifies how \"spread out\" a probability distribution is. The expected value $\\textrm E[X]$ can be interpreted as the \"center\" of a probability distribution, and the squared difference $(X - \\textrm E[X])^2$ represents the squared distance between $X$ and the center. So, the variance quantifies how far the possible values of $X$ are from the center, on average.\n\nIn general,\n\n- a probability distribution that is *spread out* over many possible values will have a *high* variance, while\n- a probability distribution that is *concentrated* (or *clumped*) near a single value will have a *low* variance." :tutorial/id #uuid "3f7e3cc9-a0b0-5b16-8e87-4f10bbf73aee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4450 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a71679e2-16b2-5111-beea-f73fd76821b5" :step/math-academy-id 27399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106766}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107106 :tutorial/content "Using the properties of the expected value of a discrete random variable $X,$ we can arrive at the following alternative formula for the variance of $X{:}$\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ X^2 ] - \\textrm E[X]^2$$\n\nAs we will see in the following example, using this formula often speeds up the process of computing the variance." :tutorial/id #uuid "ce108c7b-5e89-59e8-846f-bcf203c4ee79" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4467 :tutorial/title "A Formula for Quickly Computing the Variance"} :step/id #uuid "76a6780c-6404-599d-aa2e-2e6aa5ac7552" :step/math-academy-id 27500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105662 :question/id #uuid "fcd99105-cd96-57d0-b5df-b6230f18a181" :question/math-academy-id "e-7415"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e6a0c79-9597-5fca-9d41-305c541a7bdf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380782}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Variance Given a Probability Mass Function"} :step/id #uuid "1a2016b4-48a7-5dfc-93b3-a9521cc2b661" :step/math-academy-id 27501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105663 :question/id #uuid "f07a0b1e-2b90-5b83-b9e2-a8da0bedf044" :question/math-academy-id "e-7416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11b6d79a-b13d-5e77-899d-8b1b91c872b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382531}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Variance Given the Expected Value of a Random Variable and Its Square"} :step/id #uuid "12f495d3-24c3-58a9-82e9-2a310279a966" :step/math-academy-id 27502 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107108 :tutorial/content "The **standard deviation** of a random variable $X$ is defined as the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]}\n$$\n\nAs we will see in the future, the standard deviation is often used when we observe a random variable and wish to quantify how \"surprising\" a particular observation was.\n\nIn those contexts, we often compute how many standard deviations the observed value was away from the expected value: the further away, the more surprising the observation.\n\nFor now, though, let's get some practice computing the standard deviation of a random variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "802e056f-a5ea-57cc-bc0b-a1ff76db978f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4508 :tutorial/title "The Standard Deviation of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "69237ed9-885c-540d-8048-db64edded5dd" :step/math-academy-id 27749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107122 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following formula for the variance:\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2$$\n\nTo begin, recall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].$$\n\nExpanding out the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] =\\textrm E [ X^2 - 2\\textrm E[X] \\cdot X + \\textrm E[X]^2 ] .$$\n\nThen, distributing the expectation over the sum, we get\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ X^2 ] + \\textrm E[ - 2\\textrm E[X] \\cdot X ] + \\textrm E[ \\textrm E[X]^2 ] .$$\n\nNow, we make the following simplifications:\n\n- Since $\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X]$ for any constant $a,$ we can write $$\\textrm E[{\\color{blue}-2 \\textrm E[X]} \\cdot X] = {\\color{blue}- 2\\textrm E[X]} \\cdot \\textrm E[ X ] = - 2\\textrm E[X]^2.$$\n- Since $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is a constant, and $\\textrm E[a] = a$ for any constant $a,$ we can write $$\\textrm E[ {\\color{blue}\\textrm E[X]^2} ] = {\\color{blue}\\textrm E[X]^2}.$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[-2\\text{E}[X]\\cdot X]+\\text{E}[E[X]^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]^{2}+\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\ & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "839800ad-eaf8-58c6-b040-ec22eea833bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5259 :tutorial/title "Proving the Formula For the Variance in Terms of First and Second Raw Moments"} :step/id #uuid "8309d975-89e3-537f-8957-da5440c1379a" :step/math-academy-id 32921}] :activity/title "Variance of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106011}]}
